    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Pines Lake began in the 1920s as a vacation community around a man‑made lake about 1.5 miles long and 0.5 miles wide.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pines_Lake%2C_New_Jersey"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Many original homes were log cabins built of American chestnut in the 1930s, and many still serve as year‑round residences.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pines_Lake%2C_New_Jersey"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A one‑lane road over the dam offers a view into the Glen, a steep‑sided ravine that may hide an Indian burial ground and shows Lenape stone mounds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        12,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pines_Lake%2C_New_Jersey"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Each July, Pines Lake hosts a Baby Parade and a sold‑out Lobster Bash at the South Beach Pavilion, drawing families for a beach‑side picnic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pines_Lake%2C_New_Jersey"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The lake’s shoreline shelters rare tiger salamanders, native ferns, and occasional marine fossils found in the Glen and surrounding properties.",
